 {id:"distribution",keys:"distribution strongly agree strongly disagree neutral dissatisfied satisfied likert mean median mode rating scale",pages:[25,26],text:"Figure 7 reports 45% Agree, 9% Strongly Agree, 35% Neutral, 9% Disagree and 2% Strongly Disagree. The mean rating was 3 (Neutral), median 3.5 and mode 4 (Agree). Neutral is not the same as dissatisfied: the combined disagreement was about 11%, while positive ratings accounted for 54%."},
 {id:"association",keys:"association associations chi square bivariate significance significant p value sex age employment status",pages:[28,29],text:"The report's chi-square analysis found statistically significant associations for sex and age (both p < 0.001). Employment length (p = 0.27) and employment status (p = 0.09) were not significant in the reported bivariate analysis. These are associations, not evidence of causation."},
 {id:"regression",keys:"regression ordinal logistic predictors adjusted model odds coefficient wald confidence interval",pages:[30,31],text:"The ordinal regression model identified significant categories for sex, age, length of employment and division. Examples: female vs male B = −0.75, p < 0.001; age 18–29 vs 60+ B = −0.74, p = 0.021; less than one year of employment vs over ten years B = 1.11, p < 0.001. Table 5 also reports significant coefficients for Corporate Services, Executive Management and NPHL relative to Technical Services. These adjusted coefficients are not divisional CSAT rankings and do not establish causality."},
 {id:"limitations",keys:"limitations weakness strengths bias recall response bias representativeness causal causation generalise generalize reliability validity",pages:[55],text:"The study identifies potential response bias and recall bias because staff reported previous experiences. Strengths include stratified random selection, use of established GOJ service dimensions and a recognised CSAT calculation. A cross-sectional observational design cannot establish causality; findings describe the study period, not current performance. The response rate also needs reconciliation (68% reported; about 69% calculated)."},
 {id:"comments",keys:"open ended comments feedback qualitative survey responses praise positive negative comments",pages:[31,32],text:"96 of 229 survey respondents left comments. Table 6 classifies 15 as positive and 81 as neutral/negative (not all negative). Respondents praised professionalism and support in DIAS, the Chief Nursing Office, Epidemiology and ICTB. EEHCSD was praised for customer service and efficiency. Other comments identified communication, process delays, resource constraints, workplace infrastructure and accountability challenges."},
 {id:"procurement",keys:"procurement purchasing requisition approvals bureaucracy slow process",pages:[32,43,53,72],text:"The survey comments identified lengthy procurement processes, delayed approvals, unclear communications and workload pressures. The report proposes mapping workflows, documenting SOPs and clarifying turnaround times. A practical suggestion is to provide a request reference, an expected update date and a clear escalation route; this is a derived improvement idea, not an approved procurement rule."},
 {id:"hr",keys:"hr hrmd human resources job letter appointment orientation onboarding recruitment grievance human resource",pages:[32,54,72,73],text:"Staff reported delays in recruitment, job letters and appointments, concerns about document handling, and inadequate orientation. The discussion calls for more timely HR processes and effective onboarding. Appendix 5 proposes workflow documentation, leadership support and welfare initiatives. These are study findings, not an assessment of any individual HR officer."},
 {id:"training",keys:"emotional intelligence empathy active listening conflict resolution succession mentorship training professional development",pages:[53,70,73],text:"The discussion identifies gaps in customer service training, emotional intelligence, active listening, empathy, conflict resolution, leadership development and succession planning. The proposed response includes structured onboarding, continuing development, mentorship and better visibility of training services. Appendix 5 targets at least 85% mandatory customer service training completion."},
 {id:"facilities",keys:"mould mold air conditioning air quality pigeon parking occupational health workplace conditions infrastructure",pages:[32,52,74],text:"Staff reported concerns about workplace conditions including air quality, mould, air-conditioning and parking. The discussion highlights the need for facility maintenance and environmental assessments, while Appendix 5 proposes maintenance reporting, quarterly reviews and designated customer parking. These were reported concerns during the study, not verified current hazards."},
 {id:"leadership",keys:"leadership supervision supervisor management accountability favouritism favoritism recognition morale pmas performance appraisal",pages:[51,52,53,54,73],text:"The qualitative findings describe perceived inconsistent supervision, favouritism, insufficient guidance, accountability concerns and low morale. Participants also reported positive experiences. The report proposes leadership development, 360-degree feedback, monitored improvement plans and staff welfare and recognition initiatives. Reported perceptions should not be treated as proven misconduct."},
 {id:"communication",keys:"unanswered emails calls replies communication role ambiguity information collaboration staff directory",pages:[32,49,53,54,73],text:"Communication tied for the highest survey dimension at 59%, yet staff described unanswered messages, inadequate updates, unclear responsibilities and weak collaboration. These findings are complementary: a relative strength in the ratings can still be an important improvement priority. Appendix 5 proposes clearer communication systems and directory updates."},
 {id:"purpose",keys:"aim objective purpose justification why conducted internal customer external public patient",pages:[10,11,14,16,56],text:"The study assessed how Ministry employees experience internal services, identified challenges and proposed evidence-based improvements. It is not a patient-satisfaction survey. The objective is to strengthen internal operations and staff-centred service, which may support public health service delivery; the study does not quantify a causal effect on patients."}
